WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.160
So the first question that you
may be asking in the audience

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.230
today is, who am I?

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:15.190
I am a marine community
ecologist by training.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:18.940
I'm passionate about using math
to understand natural systems

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:20.890
and using RStudio to do it.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.650
My current work is looking
at marine communities

00:00:23.650 --> 00:00:26.230
and how they're responding
to climate change.

00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:29.770
And so even though I'm battling
a lot of imposter syndrome

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:32.800
to say this in front of the room
full of incredible R RStudio

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.490
users in front of me today.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:38.440
I am a data scientist.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:39.060
Thank you.

00:00:41.710 --> 00:00:43.210
Thank you.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:46.570
And as a data scientist
and as a marine scientist,

00:00:46.570 --> 00:00:48.580
I'm very familiar with
walking into rooms

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:51.250
and being one of
very few, if any.

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:57.130
In fact, I am one of the 3%.
Data suggests that fewer than 3%

00:00:57.130 --> 00:00:59.590
of data scientists
are women of color.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:04.000
And while I humbly stand before
you all today pronouncing this

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:07.450
as a success story,
there were a number

00:01:07.450 --> 00:01:11.150
of trials and tribulations
along the way.

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:13.100
And so I want to
talk a little bit

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:16.520
about how I was almost lost on
this journey to data science

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:18.830
and how I almost was not
standing in front of you

00:01:18.830 --> 00:01:21.050
all today giving
this presentation.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:23.120
When I started
graduate school, I

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.180
felt like the corner
of this photo,

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:28.880
of this beautiful
illustration by Alison Horst.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.780
I was in my own little corner.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:32.510
I didn't know what I was doing.

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:34.430
It felt like it was
a lot of rainy days,

00:01:34.430 --> 00:01:36.440
and I was especially
weighed down

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:39.740
by the exclusivity I
felt about data science.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.230
It felt like a tool
that I was using,

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:45.290
but it didn't feel like a
community that I was a part of.

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.560
I was also weighed down
by the historic exclusion

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:50.600
of minority STEM
professionals that I

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.840
felt very much so in academia.

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:57.440
And I also was stunned at the
lack of action and support

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:00.500
from my institution and
from academia in general

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:02.900
in the wake of a call
for social justice

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:05.720
and reform and
equity in our field.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:08.240
And so I lost my spark.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.680
It felt like I didn't know why I
was in graduate school anymore.

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:14.780
It felt like I didn't know why
I was using these tools anymore.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.350
But open science saved me.

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.210
It opened this
landscape of community.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.660
It opened this opportunity
for me to find myself again.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.310
And I found community in
RStudio and its users.

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:32.120
I also participated in the
Openscapes champions program

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:34.580
and it changed my
workflow radically.

00:02:34.580 --> 00:02:37.220
And I discovered
once again my passion

00:02:37.220 --> 00:02:40.610
for data science education
and also for engaging

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:42.530
other students as well.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.690
And so I may have
said two words already

00:02:44.690 --> 00:02:48.390
that you're unfamiliar with, so
I want to define them quickly.

00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:52.920
The first is open science,
and the second is Openscapes.

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.970
So open science is the
tools and the practices

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.280
that enable all
of us to have more

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:03.150
reproducible, transparent and
inclusive practices for data

00:03:03.150 --> 00:03:04.590
intensive science.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.330
And so when we're
talking about tools,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:09.900
we're talking about these open
source softwares and practices

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.880
that we're using,
for example, github,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.400
and we talk about the practices
as well and the communities

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.720
that exist around these tools,
for example, RStudio community.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.930
And if you had the pleasure of
hearing this morning's keynote,

00:03:21.930 --> 00:03:24.160
you learned a little
bit about Openscapes.

00:03:24.160 --> 00:03:26.970
And so Openscapes helps
researchers reimagine

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:29.850
data intensive science
and also to develop

00:03:29.850 --> 00:03:32.850
the skills to cultivate
collaborative and inclusive

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:33.670
teams.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.540
And they do that in
two ways in particular.

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:39.480
The first is through the Openscapes
champions program,

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.580
which I was a
participant in, which

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.270
is an open science mentorship
program for science teams.

00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.060
And the second is through
cultivating the Openscapes

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.960
mindset, which is a community
building framework to support

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:53.850
the transition to open science.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:55.860
And it relies on
these three principles

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.240
that turn into a cycle
of community building.

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:01.010
The first is to
empower research teams.

00:04:01.010 --> 00:04:03.360
The second is to
amplify leaders,

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.380
and the third is
to engage mentors.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:09.170
And so when I participated
in the Openscapes champions

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.510
program, it changed my life.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:15.200
I was so excited to see that
new, beautiful landscape

00:04:15.200 --> 00:04:17.930
of open science and
collaboration that existed.

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:20.959
And so I asked two questions
after my participation

00:04:20.959 --> 00:04:23.870
in Openscapes when we were
checking in a few months later.

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:28.430
The first was, why had I never
heard of open science before?

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:31.160
And the second was, how
do I get more students

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:34.010
like myself to learn
about open science

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:37.310
before they fall through
the cracks in this pipeline?

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:39.980
And so that conversation,
those questions

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:41.730
turned into a dialogue.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:45.140
And that dialogue has now turned
into a long term collaboration

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:48.350
with Openscapes to
support and explore

00:04:48.350 --> 00:04:50.960
how we can support
students from historically

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.290
excluded backgrounds on their
journeys to data science.

00:04:54.290 --> 00:04:57.020
And more than that,
it also connected me

00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:59.360
with an incredible
community of people

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.930
with similar, similarly
motivated by their values,

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:08.540
by valuing diversity, inclusion,
transparency and collaboration.

00:05:08.540 --> 00:05:11.210
And so I was all of a
sudden exposed to all

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.820
of these incredible
resources, especially the R and 

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:15.090
RStudio community.

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:18.440
And I learned what
it had to offer.

00:05:18.440 --> 00:05:21.440
And so I went from an
outsider and believing

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.970
that data science
was just a tool

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:25.530
that I used to do my work to.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.740
Now, being a participant
in the change

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:32.150
that I'm hoping to see in
our data science communities.

00:05:32.150 --> 00:05:35.240
And so when we've been
reflecting about how

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.630
I made it to this point.

00:05:36.630 --> 00:05:38.810
And how I'm standing in
front of you all today,

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.300
I thought about a formula
for the success that's

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:45.660
led to this point, the first
point being values in action.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:49.160
The second being investment
aligned with these values,

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:53.070
and the third being an Openscapes 
mindset at all levels.

00:05:53.070 --> 00:05:56.520
And I'll take a moment
to go into each of these.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:58.750
The first is values in action.

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.290
It was incredibly
refreshing for me

00:06:01.290 --> 00:06:04.710
to participate and be a part
of communities that showed me

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.490
that they were
walking their talk.

00:06:06.490 --> 00:06:10.860
Not only is inclusivity and
transparency and collaboration

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.080
a part of the work
that RStudio is doing,

00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:15.600
but it's also a part of the
community and the people

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:16.750
who are a part of it.

00:06:16.750 --> 00:06:21.620
Some examples of that include
my users and our Ladies.

00:06:21.620 --> 00:06:24.860
Also being able to
see and experience

00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:27.890
the authentic leadership within
the open science community,

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:29.640
in particular with Openscapes.

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:31.580
In our studio,
we've had the chance

00:06:31.580 --> 00:06:34.160
to hear from so many of
the incredible developers

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:36.020
and leaders who are
part of these teams,

00:06:36.020 --> 00:06:38.060
and it's refreshing
to see how open they

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:40.090
are to hearing our concerns.

00:06:40.090 --> 00:06:42.510
How much of part of they
are part of this community?

00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.350
And it matters that all of
us are working together.

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:48.830
And lastly, action with
a sense of urgency.

00:06:48.830 --> 00:06:50.870
I don't think any of
us would be here today

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:53.120
if we didn't believe that
RStudio community was going

00:06:53.120 --> 00:06:55.120
to make this a safe place.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.630
This an example of that is
the code of conduct and COVID

00:06:58.630 --> 00:07:00.670
policies for this conference.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:04.310
This is my very first time
giving a non-academic talk.

00:07:04.310 --> 00:07:06.070
This is my very
first time talking

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:09.910
about diversity and equity,
and that's very scary for me.

00:07:09.910 --> 00:07:12.010
But I knew that the
place that I wanted

00:07:12.010 --> 00:07:15.720
to do it for the first time
was at RStudio conference.

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:18.060
And that to me is
a testament to what

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:21.340
this community is capable of.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:24.580
The next is investing in
those community values.

00:07:24.580 --> 00:07:27.100
And I think that there are
two ways that the open science

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:30.280
community has demonstrated
this to me in particular.

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:33.940
And the first is prioritizing
compensating people

00:07:33.940 --> 00:07:37.060
for their time, their effort,
and their contributions

00:07:37.060 --> 00:07:39.280
to safer spaces for all of us.

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:41.830
My work with Openscapes
was the very first time

00:07:41.830 --> 00:07:44.260
in my career of working
on diversity, equity

00:07:44.260 --> 00:07:46.960
and inclusion, and over six
years where I was compensated

00:07:46.960 --> 00:07:48.550
for the work that I was doing.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:51.190
And to me, it made
me less stressed

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:53.830
that this was a side project
that I was passionate about

00:07:53.830 --> 00:07:56.358
and showed me that I could
prioritize this in my life

00:07:56.358 --> 00:07:58.150
and that it was a
priority to the community

00:07:58.150 --> 00:07:59.650
that I was serving as well.

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:01.750
And it made all the difference.

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:05.170
The next is lowering the
barriers for participation

00:08:05.170 --> 00:08:06.427
and engagement.

00:08:06.427 --> 00:08:08.260
The reason I'm standing
before you all today

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:11.230
is that I'm a participant in
the R RStudio diversity Scholars

00:08:11.230 --> 00:08:14.170
Program that made it
possible for me to be here

00:08:14.170 --> 00:08:17.320
and to participate in workshops
and present to you all today.

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.900
And I think it's just one
example of the many ways

00:08:19.900 --> 00:08:23.980
that RStudio community is
committed to making sure

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:25.840
that we get a lot of
people in these rooms,

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.940
having these conversations.

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:30.340
Additionally, the
resource guides

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:32.440
and the openness of this
community to learners

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.900
at every level is
an incredible asset

00:08:34.900 --> 00:08:37.539
to being able to bring people
from all types of backgrounds

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:39.620
into our data
science communities.

00:08:39.620 --> 00:08:42.293
I think a great example
of that is if you've ever

00:08:42.293 --> 00:08:44.710
been searching for the answer
to a question that you have,

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:49.160
you've probably stumbled upon
the rstudio blog as well.

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:52.560
And lastly is the open
scapes mindset at all levels.

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.910
As I mentioned, there are
three tiers to this flywheel.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:58.190
The first is empowering
research teams,

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:01.100
the second being amplifying
leaders, and the third

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:02.810
being engaged mentors.

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:06.800
And ultimately, what this is
building is a community where

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:09.500
at every level, regardless
of hierarchy or leadership

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:11.780
structure, that all
of us are committed

00:09:11.780 --> 00:09:13.350
to building community.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.350
All of us are committed
to mentoring and working

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:17.510
with one another.

00:09:17.510 --> 00:09:20.660
And ultimately, we're able
to build collaboration

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:25.220
and mentorship not only within
our organizations, but beyond.

00:09:25.220 --> 00:09:29.000
It makes our work more
collaborative, more inclusive.

00:09:29.000 --> 00:09:32.600
It makes it kinder and
more efficient as well.

00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.690
And ultimately, building
community with the Openscapes

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:38.490
mindset means that
you're not alone,

00:09:38.490 --> 00:09:40.700
regardless of if you
just join the team

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.720
or you're well into your work.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:45.530
This is a way for us to
collaborate and learn

00:09:45.530 --> 00:09:47.870
with one another and
no one has to try

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.390
to fix anything by themselves.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.610
And we can all
experience and enjoy

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:56.680
the landscape open
science has to offer.

00:09:56.680 --> 00:09:59.630
So what does it look
like when we do the work?

00:09:59.630 --> 00:10:02.320
What does it look like when
all of these principles

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:03.240
are in place?

00:10:03.240 --> 00:10:06.290
What does it look like when
we put this formula together?

00:10:06.290 --> 00:10:07.790
Values and action.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:10.160
Investment aligned
with these values.

00:10:10.160 --> 00:10:14.600
And lastly, an Openscapes
mindset at all levels.

00:10:14.600 --> 00:10:17.930
It looks like going
from learner to teacher.

00:10:17.930 --> 00:10:22.160
This is a photo of me teaching
my very first open science

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.370
lecture to a cohort of
Openscapes champions

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:28.620
that participated after I did.

00:10:28.620 --> 00:10:32.520
It looks like expanding our
open data and open principles

00:10:32.520 --> 00:10:34.450
to communities beyond our own.

00:10:34.450 --> 00:10:36.520
And I was able to host an event.

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:38.440
In other communities
that I'm a part of.

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:39.280
This year

00:10:39.280 --> 00:10:41.820
I hosted in a data
science mixer for Black

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.620
and marine science and
other organizations

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:44.770
that I'm a member of.

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:48.600
And I was able to bring together
quantitative marine scientists

00:10:48.600 --> 00:10:51.540
to build community and
talk about open science

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.750
and explore how we can
collaborate with one another.

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:58.140
And lastly, it looks like
building the better future

00:10:58.140 --> 00:10:59.460
that we want to see.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:03.270
This is from my colleague
and I being recipients

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:06.690
of the code for science and
society event fund grant, where

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.910
in the spring of
2023 we're going

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:10.950
to be bringing a
Pathways to open science

00:11:10.950 --> 00:11:13.350
programming to students from
historically Black colleges

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:14.550
and universities.

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:16.325
HbCUs, of which I am an alum.

00:11:18.840 --> 00:11:19.540
Thank you.

00:11:27.510 --> 00:11:31.590
And so all of that to say
that a more equitable future

00:11:31.590 --> 00:11:34.110
for our field is
possible, but it's

00:11:34.110 --> 00:11:35.790
time for us to do the work.

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.430
And so I'm leaving
everyone in this room today

00:11:38.430 --> 00:11:40.500
with three action
items and three ways

00:11:40.500 --> 00:11:43.050
to begin those conversations.

00:11:43.050 --> 00:11:46.350
And the first is the
center, the voices of those

00:11:46.350 --> 00:11:48.660
we work to support and include.

00:11:48.660 --> 00:11:50.580
This is a time to listen.

00:11:50.580 --> 00:11:52.710
This is a time to amplify.

00:11:52.710 --> 00:11:56.320
It's not a time to do
anything more than that.

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:00.310
Also to ask hard questions
in the rooms where you do

00:12:00.310 --> 00:12:01.780
have a voice.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:05.350
Look at your organization,
at your hiring structure,

00:12:05.350 --> 00:12:08.110
at your onboarding
process, and ask yourself

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.650
some of those questions.

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.980
Where are people falling
through the cracks?

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:14.420
Where can we do better?

00:12:14.420 --> 00:12:18.230
What needs to happen for
our team to include everyone

00:12:18.230 --> 00:12:20.700
at any level?

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.490
And lastly, be the
community member

00:12:23.490 --> 00:12:27.380
who you want to meet at the
beginning of your journey.

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:30.470
Had I not met Julie, I would
not be standing in front of you

00:12:30.470 --> 00:12:31.520
all today.

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:35.390
And so I encourage
you all to visit Julie

00:12:35.390 --> 00:12:39.680
and also to continue
to perpetuate community

00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:43.400
and provide this inclusive space
at every level of leadership,

00:12:43.400 --> 00:12:46.550
at every level of engagement,
so that no matter what

00:12:46.550 --> 00:12:50.600
angle someone enters RStudio
community at or enters the data

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:53.630
science community, yet
that they feel included

